/*   This test tests whether local variables are local.
  
    BUG:  The documentation is quite clear on what should be happening here
                https://www.deltatao.com/clanlord/macros/manual.html#Variables
  
        **Local Variables**
  
        These variables are defined only for the macro they are declared within. You
        declare them the same as you would a global variable, except they are within
        a macro (that is, inside the curly braces { } surrounding the macro’s contents).
  
    Now, I suppose a legal weasel might make a capital case out of the word within.
    If funcA calls funcB, maybe we might say funcB is "within" funcA? However, this
    is not the commonly understood behavior of local variables in computer science,
    something we should expect should be well known to the C++ code jockeys at DeltaTao.
    (To be clear, I am not implying DT is unwise. I am implying the original client has
    a bug, as we shall see in a moment. They would be aware of computer science mores
    and would not go against them, unless they had a good reason like historical MUD syntax
    compatibility) To programming nerds, "Within" as a matter of culture and art would be
    translated as function scope and function scope means only is visible in the function
    itself, not other things it calls or the things that call it.
  
    >The rationale for the consensus design is obvious. If subroutines can quietly modify
    caller's variables, you are going to end up with bugs. That would make it really hard
    to write libraries, for example. If two different authors are fond of returning the
    result in a variable called "result" or indexing their loops with a variable called "i",
    then we are going to have a great deal of ++unfun in certain cases where one function
    calls another in that loop. Typically, this is the reason why we _have_ local and global
    variables in the first place: to make very clear what might be changed by anyone and what
    is not going to be. The local variable is your safe place to stick stuff that nobody else
    knows about or can monkey with. In a world of code written by a million monkeys, local
    variables are the one thing known to work, because you are the only monkey who can see it.
  
    ...except in Clanlord. In the legacy client, local variables have (mostly?) global
    scope. (Clieunk is found to be bug for bug compatible with the original in this regards.)
    If you call a subroutine and it uses a local variable with the same name as the callers
    local variable, the subroutine will inherit the value of the local variable from the caller
    and when it modifies it, the change is reflected in the callers local variable, presumably
    because they are the same variable!
  
    As mentioned earlier, I, Dibbun, think this is a bug in the original implementation, which
    we will codify here in perpetuity, because fixing it "might" (it probably wont, but it "might")
    break some foolish code that relies on this behavior. Since DT no longer maintains or
    releases a Clanlord client, the opportunity to effect a fix has passed from the realm
    of possibility (for DT), so in this case, the future is unusually clear. We must put
    up with it and try to maintain compatibility. If they decided to roll a new client, they
    might fix it, but honestly I think they would be better off making use of something like
    javascript, in particular because Apple doesn't allow other scripting languages on the App
    Store, including this one. I've yet to see a page of macros that can't be rewritten in a
    day, by AI if necessary. Javascript is well understood by a lot of people. It isn't that many
    people or that much code. There is very little installed base to protect.
  
    **How to respond to this?** Right thinking macro developers will tag their local
    variables with a name space unique to the function so local variables have local
    scope as best as might be imagined. Since function names must be unique, the namespace
    if you modify the variable name with your function name, your variable name should be
    unique. In this work, we use the convention <localVarName>::<funcName> for local
    variable names, so they actually are local.
    
    > C++ would have this in the other order, <namespace>::<localVarName>. In practice, this
    works fine for C++ because local variables are almost never namespaced. You don't need to
    because they are local. I find that if we have to namespace our local variables and the
    namespaces are first, then our code gets really hard to read, because all the variables
    look the same. Swapping the order of the namespace and name makes the code more readable.
    It is even more readable if you abbreviate the functionName, but this may reduce uniqueness.
    
    If you actually want subroutines to modify caller's variables, use a global. It is common
    among programming nerds to prefix globals with the letter g (e.g. "gMyGlobalVariable") so
    that they are obviously a dangerous global wherever they are used.
*/
Test2LocalScope
{
    setglobal gLocalTestName "Test2LocalScope"

    set localVar 1
    call Test2LocalScopeAux
    setglobal gABIIntReturn localVar
}

Test2LocalScopeAux
{
    set localVar + 1
}

